      "locked": false,
      "text": "                                                                        DISTRIBUTED LOCKS\nTHE PROBLEM:\n\nYou have 10 instances of your service running simultaneously. A scheduled job must run exactly once every hour — not 10 times. Or a payment \nmust be processed exactly once even if 3 instances receive the same webhook simultaneously.\n\nOn a single machine, you use a mutex (mutual exclusion). In a distributed system, you need a distributed lock.\n\nA distributed lock (also called a distributed mutex) ensures that only one process across all instances can hold the lock at any time.\n\nTHE REQUIREMENTS:\n\nA correct distributed lock must satisfy:\n\n- Mutual exclusion: Only one process holds the lock at a time\n- Deadlock-free: If a lock holder crashes, the lock is eventually released (TTL-based)\n- Fault tolerance: The lock mechanism itself is fault-tolerant (not a single point of failure)\n\n                                                            REDIS-BASED DISTRIBUTED LOCKS\n\nRedis's single-threaded architecture makes every command atomic. The \"SET NX PX\" command atomically:\n\n- Sets a key to a value\n- Only if the key doesn't already exist (NX = Not eXists)\n- With an automatic expiry (PX = milliseconds)\n\njavascript code:\n\nconst acquireLock = async (lockKey, ttlMs) => {\n    // Unique identifier for this lock holder\n    // CRITICAL: must be unique per process/instance, not per lock\n    const lockValue = `${process.pid}:${Date.now()}:${Math.random()}`;\n\n    // SET lockKey lockValue NX PX ttlMs\n    // Returns \"OK\" if acquired, null if key already exists\n    const result = await redis.set(lockKey, lockValue, 'NX', 'PX', ttlMs);\n\n    if (result === 'OK') {\n        return lockValue; // return identifier so we can verify on release\n    }\n    return null; // lock is held by someone else\n};\n\nconst releaseLock = async (lockKey, lockValue) => {\n    // CRITICAL: Use Lua script for atomic check-and-delete\n    // Without this, there's a race condition:\n    // 1. We check if value matches (it does)\n    // 2. Our TTL expires, lock is auto-released\n    // 3. Another process acquires the lock\n    // 4. We delete the key — but we're now deleting THEIR lock!\n    const script = `\n        if redis.call(\"get\", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then\n            return redis.call(\"del\", KEYS[1])\n        else\n            return 0\n        end\n    `;\n\n    return await redis.eval(script, 1, lockKey, lockValue);\n};\n\n// Usage\nconst processPayment = async (paymentId) => {\n    const lockKey = `payment:${paymentId}:lock`;\n    const lockTTL = 30000; // 30 seconds — must be > max expected processing time\n\n    const lock = await acquireLock(lockKey, lockTTL);\n\n    if (!lock) {\n        // Another instance is processing this payment\n        console.log(`Payment ${paymentId} already being processed`);\n        return;\n    }\n\n    try {\n        await doPaymentProcessing(paymentId);\n    } finally {\n        // Always release the lock, even on error\n        await releaseLock(lockKey, lock);\n    }\n};\n\n\n                                        THE CRITICAL BUG: TTL MUST BE GREATER THAN PROCESSING TIME\n\nWrong TTL:\n  Process acquires lock with TTL = 5 seconds\n  Processing takes 8 seconds\n  At 5 seconds: TTL expires → lock auto-released\n  At 5.001 seconds: Another process acquires the lock\n  At 5.002 seconds: BOTH processes hold the lock simultaneously\n  → Mutual exclusion violated\n\nThe TTL must be long enough that processing always completes before expiry, but short enough that failures are detected in a reasonable time. \nThis is a fundamental tension.\n\nSOLUTION: Lock extension. While processing, periodically extend the lock TTL:\n\njavascript code:\n\nconst processWithLockExtension = async (lockKey, lockValue, processFn) => {\n    // Extend lock every 10 seconds while processing\n    const extendInterval = setInterval(async () => {\n        const extended = await redis.eval(`\n            if redis.call(\"get\", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then\n                return redis.call(\"pexpire\", KEYS[1], ARGV[2])\n            else\n                return 0\n            end\n        `, 1, lockKey, lockValue, 30000); // extend by 30 more seconds\n\n        if (!extended) {\n            // We lost the lock (Redis restarted, TTL expired, etc.)\n            // Must abort processing\n            throw new Error('Lost distributed lock during processing');\n        }\n    }, 10000);\n\n    try {\n        return await processFn();\n    } finally {\n        clearInterval(extendInterval);\n    }\n};\n\n                                                        THE REDLOCK ALGORITHM — MULTI-NODE LOCKS\n\nA single Redis instance is a single point of failure. If Redis goes down, no locks can be acquired (if you fail safely) or all locks are released simul-\ntaneously (if Redis just restarts).\n\nMartin Kleppmann (author of \"Designing Data-Intensive Applications\") and Redis creator Salvatore Sanfilippo had a famous debate about this. \nRedlock is Redis's answer to fault-tolerant locking.\n\nRedlock uses 5 independent Redis instances (not replicas — independent master nodes). A lock is acquired by:\n\n- Get current timestamp T1\n- Try to acquire the lock on ALL 5 nodes simultaneously (using the single-node algorithm)\n- Count how many nodes responded OK\n- Check: did we get > N/2 nodes (majority = 3 of 5)?\n- Check: did it all happen within the validity time? (T2 - T1 < TTL)\n- If both: lock is acquired. Effective TTL = original TTL - (T2 - T1)\n- If not: release the lock on all nodes that granted it. Try again.\n\njavascript code:\n\nconst redlock = new Redlock(\n    [redis1, redis2, redis3, redis4, redis5],\n    { retryCount: 3, retryDelay: 200 }\n);\n\nconst lock = await redlock.acquire(['payment:123:lock'], 30000);\ntry {\n    await processPayment('123');\n} finally {\n    await lock.release();\n}\n\n\nWhy majority matters: Even if 2 of 5 nodes are down, you can still get 3/5 votes. The lock is valid because a majority agrees. The 2 down nodes \ncan't grant the lock to anyone else either — they're down. When they come back, their locks have expired (TTL). So at most one client holds the \nlock at any time.\n\n                                                        DISTRIBUTED LOCKS VS OPTIMISTIC LOCKING (I NEED TO LOOK INTO THESE BETTER)\n                                          Distributed Lock                                           Optimistic Locking\nWhen to use          Long-running critical sections, external side effects        Short database operations, high-read, low-conflict\nMechanism                Explicit lock acquire/release                                                 Version check at commit time\nFailure mode              Deadlock if not released                                                        Retry on conflict\nPerformance             Serializes access (slow under high contention)                          Fast if conflicts are rare\nExternal effects        Can hold the lock while calling external APIs           Can't use for operations that have external side effects\n\nUse distributed locks when you need to:\n\nPrevent concurrent execution of code that has side effects outside the database\nCoordinate across multiple service instances\n\nUse optimistic locking when:\n\nYour critical section is just a database read-modify-write\nConflicts are rare\nRetrying on conflict is cheap",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                                                                        DISTRIBUTED LOCKS\nTHE PROBLEM:\n\nYou have 10 instances of your service running simultaneously. A scheduled job must run exactly once every hour — not 10 times. Or a payment \nmust be processed exactly once even if 3 instances receive the same webhook simultaneously.\n\nOn a single machine, you use a mutex (mutual exclusion). In a distributed system, you need a distributed lock.\n\nA distributed lock (also called a distributed mutex) ensures that only one process across all instances can hold the lock at any time.\n\nTHE REQUIREMENTS:\n\nA correct distributed lock must satisfy:\n\n- Mutual exclusion: Only one process holds the lock at a time\n- Deadlock-free: If a lock holder crashes, the lock is eventually released (TTL-based)\n- Fault tolerance: The lock mechanism itself is fault-tolerant (not a single point of failure)\n\n                                                            REDIS-BASED DISTRIBUTED LOCKS\n\nRedis's single-threaded architecture makes every command atomic. The \"SET NX PX\" command atomically:\n\n- Sets a key to a value\n- Only if the key doesn't already exist (NX = Not eXists)\n- With an automatic expiry (PX = milliseconds)\n\njavascript code:\n\nconst acquireLock = async (lockKey, ttlMs) => {\n    // Unique identifier for this lock holder\n    // CRITICAL: must be unique per process/instance, not per lock\n    const lockValue = `${process.pid}:${Date.now()}:${Math.random()}`;\n\n    // SET lockKey lockValue NX PX ttlMs\n    // Returns \"OK\" if acquired, null if key already exists\n    const result = await redis.set(lockKey, lockValue, 'NX', 'PX', ttlMs);\n\n    if (result === 'OK') {\n        return lockValue; // return identifier so we can verify on release\n    }\n    return null; // lock is held by someone else\n};\n\nconst releaseLock = async (lockKey, lockValue) => {\n    // CRITICAL: Use Lua script for atomic check-and-delete\n    // Without this, there's a race condition:\n    // 1. We check if value matches (it does)\n    // 2. Our TTL expires, lock is auto-released\n    // 3. Another process acquires the lock\n    // 4. We delete the key — but we're now deleting THEIR lock!\n    const script = `\n        if redis.call(\"get\", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then\n            return redis.call(\"del\", KEYS[1])\n        else\n            return 0\n        end\n    `;\n\n    return await redis.eval(script, 1, lockKey, lockValue);\n};\n\n// Usage\nconst processPayment = async (paymentId) => {\n    const lockKey = `payment:${paymentId}:lock`;\n    const lockTTL = 30000; // 30 seconds — must be > max expected processing time\n\n    const lock = await acquireLock(lockKey, lockTTL);\n\n    if (!lock) {\n        // Another instance is processing this payment\n        console.log(`Payment ${paymentId} already being processed`);\n        return;\n    }\n\n    try {\n        await doPaymentProcessing(paymentId);\n    } finally {\n        // Always release the lock, even on error\n        await releaseLock(lockKey, lock);\n    }\n};\n\n\n                                        THE CRITICAL BUG: TTL MUST BE GREATER THAN PROCESSING TIME\n\nWrong TTL:\n  Process acquires lock with TTL = 5 seconds\n  Processing takes 8 seconds\n  At 5 seconds: TTL expires → lock auto-released\n  At 5.001 seconds: Another process acquires the lock\n  At 5.002 seconds: BOTH processes hold the lock simultaneously\n  → Mutual exclusion violated\n\nThe TTL must be long enough that processing always completes before expiry, but short enough that failures are detected in a reasonable time. \nThis is a fundamental tension.\n\nSOLUTION: Lock extension. While processing, periodically extend the lock TTL:\n\njavascript code:\n\nconst processWithLockExtension = async (lockKey, lockValue, processFn) => {\n    // Extend lock every 10 seconds while processing\n    const extendInterval = setInterval(async () => {\n        const extended = await redis.eval(`\n            if redis.call(\"get\", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then\n                return redis.call(\"pexpire\", KEYS[1], ARGV[2])\n            else\n                return 0\n            end\n        `, 1, lockKey, lockValue, 30000); // extend by 30 more seconds\n\n        if (!extended) {\n            // We lost the lock (Redis restarted, TTL expired, etc.)\n            // Must abort processing\n            throw new Error('Lost distributed lock during processing');\n        }\n    }, 10000);\n\n    try {\n        return await processFn();\n    } finally {\n        clearInterval(extendInterval);\n    }\n};\n\n                                                        THE REDLOCK ALGORITHM — MULTI-NODE LOCKS\n\nA single Redis instance is a single point of failure. If Redis goes down, no locks can be acquired (if you fail safely) or all locks are released simul-\ntaneously (if Redis just restarts).\n\nMartin Kleppmann (author of \"Designing Data-Intensive Applications\") and Redis creator Salvatore Sanfilippo had a famous debate about this. \nRedlock is Redis's answer to fault-tolerant locking.\n\nRedlock uses 5 independent Redis instances (not replicas — independent master nodes). A lock is acquired by:\n\n- Get current timestamp T1\n- Try to acquire the lock on ALL 5 nodes simultaneously (using the single-node algorithm)\n- Count how many nodes responded OK\n- Check: did we get > N/2 nodes (majority = 3 of 5)?\n- Check: did it all happen within the validity time? (T2 - T1 < TTL)\n- If both: lock is acquired. Effective TTL = original TTL - (T2 - T1)\n- If not: release the lock on all nodes that granted it. Try again.\n\njavascript code:\n\nconst redlock = new Redlock(\n    [redis1, redis2, redis3, redis4, redis5],\n    { retryCount: 3, retryDelay: 200 }\n);\n\nconst lock = await redlock.acquire(['payment:123:lock'], 30000);\ntry {\n    await processPayment('123');\n} finally {\n    await lock.release();\n}\n\n\nWhy majority matters: Even if 2 of 5 nodes are down, you can still get 3/5 votes. The lock is valid because a majority agrees. The 2 down nodes \ncan't grant the lock to anyone else either — they're down. When they come back, their locks have expired (TTL). So at most one client holds the \nlock at any time.\n\n                                                        DISTRIBUTED LOCKS VS OPTIMISTIC LOCKING (I NEED TO LOOK INTO THESE BETTER)\n                                          Distributed Lock                                           Optimistic Locking\nWhen to use          Long-running critical sections, external side effects        Short database operations, high-read, low-conflict\nMechanism                Explicit lock acquire/release                                                 Version check at commit time\nFailure mode              Deadlock if not released                                                        Retry on conflict\nPerformance             Serializes access (slow under high contention)                          Fast if conflicts are rare\nExternal effects        Can hold the lock while calling external APIs           Can't use for operations that have external side effects\n\nUse distributed locks when you need to:\n\nPrevent concurrent execution of code that has side effects outside the database\nCoordinate across multiple service instances\n\nUse optimistic locking when:\n\nYour critical section is just a database read-modify-write\nConflicts are rare\nRetrying on conflict is cheap",
